## Overview
The program creates Args for each thread in each level till it reaches the aimed level, 
each thread calls my_mergesort recursively and waits till the end child thread finishes merge sort, 
then merge two child threads till level 0 thread.

## Manifest
mergesort.c		-> implementation of using thread with merge sort algorithm
mergesort.h		-> header file for mergesort.c
test-mergesort.c 	-> main file to call mergesort and check correctness

## Building the project
Makefile to  build executable:
Executable: test-mergesort
Included File: test-mergesort.o mergesort.o
Compiler Option: -Wall -Wpointer-arith -Wstrict-prototypes -std=gnu89 -fPIC -MMD -MP -lpthread
Compiler: gcc

Run executable:
./test-mergesort <input size> <cutoff level> <seed>
Example: ./test-mergesort 100000 1 1234


Libraries:
#Include <stdio.h>
This library allows the program to read input from the user for the input size, 
cutoff level and seed. It also allows for output to the terminal to prompt for input. 

#Include <string.h>
Use memcpy from the library for memory transportation when merging

#include <stdlib.h>
Use malloc from the library for pre-create spaces for Argses

No action needed to install library, it’s all included inside C Standard library


## Features and usage
mergesort.c
merge()		 	-> receive two arrays range, combine them with sorting order
my_mergesort() 	-> receive the range of array, recursively divide array into two till only one element left
parallel_mergesort()	-> keep recursively create thread till reach level or hit thread create fail, and call mergesort
buildArgs()		-> make address location for Args and create an arg

To use the program, first build with 
sudo apt update && sudo apt install -y build-essential
make 
Then run
./test-mergesort <input size><cutoff level><seed>, for example ./test-mergesort 10000000 0 1234

