and therefore crash. This was fixed via code review and communicating between myself and another group member. 

As stated previously, the most challenging part of this project for me was understanding the merge sort, 
not only the way the sort itself worked, but the way we could then implement concurrency. It clicked for me how
concurrency can be used in a system to improve performance. By allocating threads to each merge split, it 
increases the speed and the efficiency of the system. If we ensure these threads are implemented correctly and 
never come into a position of deadlock. The testing and development was quite seamless for me, especially when
working with peers that had a greater understanding of the sorting algorithm. 

Dragan: 
While working on our solution, no major bugs were detected. Before starting the assignment in earnest I had 
anticipated we may have needed to implement locks or other concurrency solutions to prevent things like 
deadlocks, however we ultimately found out that the setup of the mergesort itself excluded the possibility of 
these issues. 

Overall, the project was not particularly challenging to me as I had some familiarity with the concepts of a 
mergesort, having taken data structures and algorithms courses in the past. I did initially need to wrap my head 
around the idea of concurrency and how it was integrated into the mergesort solution, and how each split of the 
array was essentially its own thread (parent) with child threads and so on. But once I grasped this initial 
relationship the setup was fairly straightforward and intuitive, especially when we realised that deadlocks were 
essentially impossible and not something we’d have to account for. During development we did encounter a 
segmentation fault where a while loop could go beyond the bounds of the array. By working with my team members 
