together.

Chenliang Sun:
During the assignment, we did not face any essential issues. I was expecting to encounter race conditions during
the project, but once I started doing normal mergesort, we found out that the mergesort itself already prevented
the race condition from happening. The only thing we should be aware of is where to split two arrays to prevent
collision. The one error I found is when the cutoff cutoff gets bigger, for example 20, pthread_create() may 
fail, which causes pthread_join to keep waiting for two ghost threads, causing silent failure. I then added 
two conditions for both t1 and t2 threads to check whether they create a thread or not, and if they fail, they
will enter mergesort directly, without creating any further args or thread that will also fail.

I do not find anything challenging, since I’m taking parallel distributed computing (PDC) simultaneously, I have 
a more clear understanding of what concurrency is and what is happening with the program. The development and 
testing is fun, since in the early data & algorithm course I only program it without thread involvement, which is
boring. Adding threads gave me more imagination about how performance improvement works, or how computers 
process multiple	 units within a game. The overall assignment is simple for me.

